"Computation and Language (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new)\nciteturn41200view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn41184view0\",\"lineno\":250}); Total lines: 2886\nL232: Personally Identifiable Information (PII) detection models must efficiently find identifiers across diverse contexts while maintaining a low false-positive rate (FPR) on high-volume traffic. Recent detectors increasingly rely on complex architectures that raise deployment cost, yet many are fine-tuned on only a few synthetic datasets that limit their coverage.\nL233: Combining public datasets is challenging because their taxonomies differ, and even after categories are mapped to a common schema, labels can disagree on what counts as a detection, producing inconsistent training and evaluation signals. To address these problems, we present a practical 141M-parameter encoder-based detector trained on 1.12 million examples from 77 public datasets, relabeled under a detailed definition of broad contextual detection for 12 structured PII categories.\nL234: Evaluated on the AI4Privacy and Nemotron-PII benchmarks at a low-FPR operating point, our model achieves the highest recall against larger baselines in 11 of 14 comparisons under our labels and 9 of 14 under the original labels. Its largest recall gains include useful additional coverage, such as multilingual disclosures and IP addresses embedded in URLs.\nL235: It achieves this with 2-10x higher throughput and 94-97% less peak GPU memory than the baselines, delivering leading detection performance at substantially lower cost.\nL236: [18] cite161†arXiv:2610.22615 [cite162†pdf , cite163†html , cite164†other ]\nL237: \nL238: \nL239: Title: When Does Uncertainty Measure RAG Hallucination Risk?\nL240: \nL241: cite165†Rajeel Ahmad Ansari , cite166†Tautik Agrahari L242: \nL243: Comments: 6 pages. Accepted for oral presentation at ICNLSP 2026\nL244: \nL245: Journal-ref: Proceedings of the 9th International Conference on Natural Language and Speech Processing (ICNLSP-2026), pp. 53-58, 2026\nL246: \nL247: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL248: Uncertainty can measure hallucination risk in retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), but only when retrieved evidence constrains the answer. When evidence is present, unsupported factual claims become less likely; when retrieval coverage is poor, the model can answer from internal knowledge and assign high probability to unsupported claims. We test this across four instruction-tuned models on HotpotQA and SQuAD.\nL249: After matching faithful and hallucinated answers within word-count bins, length-only classifiers fall near chance, while perplexity reaches 0.81--0.91 AUROC on targeted unsupported edits. Varying retrieval depth shows why the score changes meaning: detection can weaken with only the top-ranked passage, then recover after adding a second passage. A combined uncertainty and semantic-drift score improves calibration, and measurements remain above chance on RAGTruth QA.\nL250: Results support a conditional view of uncertainty as a useful measure of RAG hallucination risk.\nL251: [19] cite167†arXiv:2610.22618 [cite168†pdf , cite169†html , cite170†other ]\nL252: \nL253: \nL254: Title: Trajectory-Guided Steering: Mitigating Spurious Feature Bias in Clinical Classification\nL255: \nL256: cite152†Fardin Ahsan Sakib , cite153†Ziwei Zhu , cite154†Ozlem Uzuner L257: \nL258: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL259: Large language models rely on spurious features in clinical classification: swapping a single gender token in clinical notes alters gender-irrelevant diagnoses in up to 53.5% of cases. Existing inference-time corrections either need counterfactual data built around the specific spurious feature or behave unpredictably. We observe that how a model's preference between answers evolves across layers, its prediction trajectory, indicates for each document whether a correction is likely to help.\nL260: We propose Trajectory-Guided Steering (TGS), which decides from the trajectory alone whether to correct each prediction and in which direction, with no counterfactual data and no knowledge of the spurious feature. On 16 clinical classification tasks from i2b2, TGS fixes 17.7% of errors while breaking only 1.3% of correct predictions and stays net-positive across all tested hyperparameter settings; all baselines are net-negative or near zero.\nL261: The same corrections reduce reliance on the spurious feature: on the most affected diseases, gender-swap inconsistency falls sharply (hypertension 53.5% -> 38.0%, diabetes 35.1% -> 21.5%). Applying the correction to every document instead raises breaks on correct positive predictions from 0.1% to 6.4%, so per-document selection is essential. TGS also transfers to three model families and three other clinical and biomedical datasets.\nL262: [20] cite171†arXiv:2610.22631 [cite172†pdf , cite173†html , cite174†other ]\nL263: \nL264: \nL265: Title: A Plausible Explanation Is Enough to Mislead Multimodal Judges\nL266: \nL267: cite175†Anglin Cai , cite22†Zhiqi Wang , cite176†Xiaozhi Yang , cite177†Yurun Lin , cite178†Dongwon Lee , cite179†Yuchen Yang L268: \nL269: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL270: Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) increasingly serve as judges in training and evaluation pipelines, often assessing answers together with explanations. Although AI judges are sensitive to textual factors, it remains unclear whether ordinary plausible explanations can sway judgments requiring visual verification. We introduce JUPE, a visual-question-answering (VQA)-style benchmark that fixes answers while varying explanations.\nL271: Across 14 MLLM judges, ordinary explanations that sound plausible mislead judges, with effects comparable on average to deliberately persuasive arguments. Plain justifications and chain-of-thought rationales reduce rejection of incorrect answers by 12.7 and 13.8 percentage points, respectively. We call this failure plausibility capture. Using signal detection theory, we find a significant mean shift in decision criterion toward acceptance, without a clear change in mean discriminability.\nL272: Explanations sway judgments across domains, explanation generators, and judging protocols, while tested mitigations offer limited, model-dependent protection. Such shifts can inflate performance estimates when judges evaluate answers with explanations.\nL273: [21] cite180†arXiv:2610.22632 [cite181†pdf , cite182†html , cite183†other ]\nL274: \nL275: \nL276: Title: ToMStories: Interpreting Theory-of-Mind in Synthetic Narratives\nL277: \nL278: cite184†Melanie Subbiah , cite185†Chia-En Jacklyn Tsai , cite186†Kathleen McKeown L279: \nL280: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL281: Understanding people often requires inferring what they feel or want from how they behave rather than from what they state outright. We ask whether language models can make these inferences, and how they do so. We introduce Theory-of-Mind Stories (ToMStories), an evaluation dataset of ~6,000 short synthetically generated narrative vignettes, each paired with a multiple-choice or open-ended question about a character's emotion, fear, motivation, or emotional wound.\nL282: These psychological states are conveyed implicitly (showing), explicitly (telling), or with conflict between dialogue and description. Evaluating thirteen open-weight models (1B-72B parameters), we find that the largest models match humans on multiple choice but not open-ended questions.\nL283: Our analyses point to simplifications models make in both interpretation and generation of implicit psychological states, indicating that synthetic data can help us understand how models perform interpretation, but likely overestimates how well models interpret humans.\nL284: [22] cite187†arXiv:2610.22636 [cite188†pdf , cite189†html , cite190†other ]\nL285: \nL286: \nL287: Title: Measuring Mechanistic Uncertainty in Large Language Models via Causal Robustness\nL288: \nL289: cite191†Aneesh Komanduri , cite192†Majid Afshar , cite193†Timothy A. Miller , cite194†Yanjun Gao L290: \nL291: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL292: Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used in critical domains, where their susceptibility to hallucinations makes reliable and interpretable uncertainty quantification essential. We introduce the concept of mechanistic uncertainty, which characterizes uncertainty through the stability of the internal mechanisms supporting a model's response.\nL293: We propose the causal robustness margin, defined as the minimum internal intervention required to deviate the model's output, and operationalize it using Jacobian Lens (Jlens) concept steering vectors. Across three open-source models and five question-answering datasets, we show incorrect and uncertain responses exhibit smaller margins, indicating greater sensitivity to internal interventions, and the causal robustness margin complements conventional output-based uncertainty methods.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputation and Language (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new)\nciteturn41200view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn41184view0\",\"lineno\":500}); Total lines: 2886\nL484: Moreover, we demonstrate that emerging \\emph{thinking models} with extended reasoning capabilities are nearly immune to position bias, suggesting a costly but general approach toward resolving this vulnerability.\nL485: [39] cite344†arXiv:2610.22810 [cite345†pdf , cite346†html , cite347†other ]\nL486: \nL487: \nL488: Title: Watermarkability: Measuring and Improving Language-Model Compatibility with Logit-Based Watermarks\nL489: \nL490: cite348†Zewei Deng , cite349†Mohamed Seif , cite350†Liyan Xie L491: \nL492: Comments: 16 pages, 2 figures, 10 tables. Code available at cite351†this https URL†github.com L493: \nL494: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\nL495: Language-model watermarks can help identify model-generated text, encode provenance information, and detect post-generation edits. A broad class of logit-based watermarks increases the logits of a designated token set at each decoding step. Because a base model's native probability mass on this set varies across contexts, models, and watermark rules, the same logit bias can yield different adherence rates and increases in perplexity (PPL).\nL496: We introduce watermarkability to characterize the compatibility between a model and the token sets requested by a watermark. At a target mass $\\tau$, we quantify this property through a watermarkability cost: the expected minimum KL divergence from the base-model distribution required to assign probability at least $\\tau$ to the requested set. A lower cost means that the model is more watermarkable for that request distribution. To reduce this cost, we propose a two-stage method.\nL497: In the first stage, we train a target-set-conditioned low-rank adapter on unions drawn from a 16-way vocabulary partition to increase the requested set's probability mass. In the second stage, a closed-form controller applies the smallest remaining uniform logit bias needed to reach the target mass. We evaluate the method on six watermark schedules using OPT-125M and Qwen3-8B.\nL498: Across the 12 model-schedule pairs, the pre-adaptation watermarkability cost correlates with the log-PPL increase under a near-hard fixed-bias baseline (pooled Pearson $r=0.929$). For every schedule, at least 99.80% of sampled tokens fall in the target set requested at their decoding step. At this reliability, the adapter and controller reduce average per-schedule prompt-conditional PPL by 46.3% on OPT-125M and 5.2% on Qwen3-8B relative to the fixed-bias baseline.\nL499: [40] cite352†arXiv:2610.22820 [cite353†pdf , cite354†html , cite355†other ]\nL500: \nL501: \nL502: Title: Causal Edit Serialization\nL503: \nL504: cite356†Louis Robinson L505: \nL506: Comments: 37 pages, including appendices and checklist. Code: cite357†this https URL†github.com ; data and checkpoints: cite358†this https URL†huggingface.co L507: \nL508: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL509: Standard autoregressive language models generate text sequentially, but revision requires referring back to tokens in the input context or those already generated. Causal Edit Serialization (CES) is introduced as a framework for expressing such revisions within the same causal token stream. Special pointer tokens are paired with predicted source addresses, allowing insertion, deletion, replacement, copying and movement to be localized explicitly.\nL510: A lightweight localization head and dual-index rotary position encoding support both address prediction and conditioning on previously emitted pointers, while retaining a standard causal attention mask. A pretraining procedure combines ordinary language modeling with serialized repairs derived from random corruptions, model-generated edits and repeated text.\nL511: Across model scales and editing baselines, CES models demonstrate complete applied edits with modestly higher clean-text bits per byte than clean autoregressive models. Supervised and reinforcement-learning post-training provide a grammatical-error-correction proof of concept, producing more beneficial than harmful changes under reference-blind model judging. Correct repair programs also improve subsequent text prediction while the original context and its cached states are retained.\nL512: CES provides a common interface for generation and localized revision, with potential extensions to iterative refinement and additional pointer-addressed transformations.\nL513: [41] cite359†arXiv:2610.22841 [cite360†pdf , cite361†html , cite362†other ]\nL514: \nL515: \nL516: Title: The Reliance Hypothesis: Chain-of-Thought Faithfulness Rises as Language Resource Level Falls\nL517: \nL518: cite363†Lucas Resck , cite364†Jingyi Sun , cite365†Qianli Wang , cite366†Isabelle Augenstein , cite367†Anna Korhonen L519: \nL520: Comments: 21 pages, 10 figures, 16 tables\nL521: \nL522: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL523: Chain-of-Thought (CoT) faithfulness matters for monitoring, explaining and safely deploying large language models, yet it has been studied mostly in English. Languages differ in level, the amount of text available to learn from, so models know less in lower-resource languages, which may change how much they rely on their reasoning. To our knowledge, we are the first to relate CoT faithfulness to both resource level and task type (knowledge- or reasoning-intensive).\nL524: On parallel multiple-choice questions from Multilingual Massive Multitask Language Understanding (MMMLU) in eight languages, faithfulness rises as resource level falls on knowledge-intensive tasks but not on reasoning-intensive ones, a gap that points the same way across four models and several faithfulness measures. We propose, and find support for, the Reliance Hypothesis: models rely more on their reasoning when they cannot recall the answer, as in lower-resource languages on knowledge-intensive tasks.\nL525: Model confidence partly explains the effect of resource level, but not the knowledge-reasoning gap. Languages in which models are less confident rely more on reasoning, yet less confident answers within a language do not, a Simpson's paradox. In lower-resource languages, models rely on their reasoning for more answers, not more strongly for each. CoT monitoring may therefore be most informative in lower-resource languages, where it is least often validated.\nL526: [42] cite368†arXiv:2610.22858 [cite369†pdf , cite370†html , cite371†other ]\nL527: \nL528: \nL529: Title: Uncertainty-Aware Budget Allocation for Efficient LLM Tree Search\nL530: \nL531: cite372†Jianguo Huang , cite373†Zhiyi Lyu , cite374†Fuxiang Zhang , cite375†Yanchen Deng , cite376†Deheng Ye , cite377†Bo An L532: \nL533: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL534: Tree search enhances multi-step reasoning in large language models (LLMs), yet prevailing methods expand every step with a fixed budget, irrespective of the marginal utility of expansion. We first show, across existing search algorithms, that continuations sampled from a shared intermediate state are largely duplicates and collapse to a few distinct answers.\nL535: Critically, we find that the semantic dispersion of sibling actions predicts the redundancy of their downstream continuations, permitting a subtree's budget to be fixed before expansion. An information-theoretic analysis motivates this: treating the per-step budget as the Monte Carlo sample size for estimating a step's residual answer entropy, we derive a sufficient budget that shrinks with sibling dispersion.\nL536: We accordingly propose $\\textbf{Uncertainty-Aware Allocation}$ (UAA), a training-free framework that decouples value from uncertainty: values determine which candidates are promising, while uncertainty, estimated from sibling dispersion at no additional model cost, determines how many merit expansion, via uncertainty-aware pruning and uncertainty-aware budgeting.\nL537: Across mathematical reasoning, logical reasoning, and planning benchmarks, UAA reduces token usage by up to $90\\%$ and latency by up to $9\\times$ at comparable accuracy, generalizing across four tree search algorithms and both open- and closed-source LLMs.\nL538: [43] cite378†arXiv:2610.22874 [cite379†pdf , cite380†html , cite381†other ]\nL539: \nL540: \nL541: Title: Identify, Classify, Act: Per-Instruction Controlled Compliance for LLMs over External Data\nL542: \nL543: cite382†Zesheng Shi , cite383†Xue Li , cite384†Chenrui Dai , cite254†Jing Li L544: \nL545: Journal-ref: EMNLP2026\nL546: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputation and Language (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new)\nciteturn41200view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn41184view0\",\"lineno\":750}); Total lines: 2886\nL725: By revisiting cross-layer sparsity from the perspective of attention dynamics, we find that inter-layer salient token shift is highly localized: although the exact top-$\\kappa$ indices differ across layers, the core high-attention tokens remain stable, with changes concentrated near the expanded boundary of previously selected regions. Motivated by this, we propose Prism Attention, a proposal-refined sparse attention framework.\nL726: It replaces rigid cross-layer index reuse with a coarse-to-fine strategy, preserving layer-wise adaptability while retaining the efficiency benefits of cross-layer sharing. Extensive experiments show that Prism Attention achieves state-of-the-art performance among mainstream sparse attention methods on both reasoning and long-context benchmarks, while attaining a $3.4\\times$ end-to-end decoding speedup over FlashAttention at 128K context length.\nL727: [58] cite512†arXiv:2610.22999 [cite513†pdf , cite514†html , cite515†other ]\nL728: \nL729: \nL730: Title: Controlling the Diversity-Quality Trade-Off of LLMs by Merging Models Before and After Alignment\nL731: \nL732: cite516†Yasuaki Sumita , cite517†Tomoharu Iwata , cite518†Toshiyuki Tanaka L733: \nL734: Comments: 13 pages, 5 figures\nL735: \nL736: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL737: Alignment is known to change the output characteristics of large language models (LLMs). Base models tend to generate more diverse outputs, whereas aligned models tend to produce higher-quality outputs. The appropriate balance between the two depends on the task. Existing methods either require costly fine-tuning or, like temperature sampling, mainly improve lexical diversity rather than semantic diversity.\nL738: We introduce a depth-aware FrankenMerging approach for controlling the diversity--quality trade-off between an aligned model and its base model without additional training. This approach combines the lower layers of an aligned model with the higher layers of its base model based on the hypothesis that lexical diversity is more closely associated with lower layers and semantic diversity with higher layers.\nL739: Experiments showed that varying the number of layers taken from each model adjusts both diversity and quality. In most cases, the merged models exhibited higher semantic diversity than the aligned model, and at some split positions, they showed higher lexical diversity than both original models. They also demonstrated a better diversity--quality trade-off than temperature sampling in most settings.\nL740: A supporting ablation, in which we reversed the merging order, yielded results largely consistent with the hypothesis.\nL741: [59] cite519†arXiv:2610.23060 [cite520†pdf , cite521†html , cite522†other ]\nL742: \nL743: \nL744: Title: Same Person, Different Advice: Auditing LLM Financial Advice with Repeated Risk-Tolerance Responses\nL745: \nL746: cite523†Soovadeep Bakshi , cite524†Apurba Bose , cite524†Aishwarya Bose , cite525†Sandip Nandi L747: \nL748: Comments: 23 pages, 5 figures, 9 tables\nL749: \nL750: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Computers and Society (cs.CY)\nL751: AI financial advisers set a client's stock allocation largely from one stated-risk question, yet people do not answer it the same way twice: in a national US panel, 41% of households chose a different answer two years later, and in a laboratory retest a third of people moved two or more points on a 0-10 scale after six months. Seven large language models from five providers gave one-turn allocation advice for 800 real household profiles in which only the risk answer changed.\nL752: The recommended stock share moved by 10 to 16 percentage points on average, and by 24 to 35 when the answer changed, against 0.6 to 4.2 points for the same prompt asked twice; in the laboratory retest the rating alone moved advice by 11 to 18 points. Without any note, the models respond to the answer about as strongly as when told that it is very reliable (less so for the two Claude models).\nL753: If that instructed response is how each model maps attitude to advice, discounting the answer for its measured reliability, as classical test theory prescribes, would cut the swings by a factor of 2.4 to 3.3. The swing is largest for clients under 40. A note that the answer is noisy steadies some models, but shifts their weight to the client's age and pulls cautious clients towards a generic middle allocation: clients unwilling to take any risk are advised 22% stocks instead of 4%.\nL754: Three extra money questions reduce the swing for some models, with no statistically detectable change relative to how much advice differs across people. In a scripted test, one clarifying question removes nearly all of the swing from an answer the client then corrects (33.5 points to 1.9 on average) while keeping the response to a change the client confirms.\nL755: We argue that same-person retest audits should be a standard check for AI advisers, and that what to do with an unreliable answer is a question of client autonomy as well as of statistics.\nL756: [60] cite526†arXiv:2610.23062 [cite527†pdf , cite528†html , cite529†other ]\nL757: \nL758: \nL759: Title: ParetoChat: An LLM-Based Interface for Interactive Multi-Objective Optimization\nL760: \nL761: cite530†Gokhan Ceyhan L762: \nL763: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Optimization and Control (math.OC)\nL764: Multi-objective optimization models are often deployed in settings where decision makers (DMs) must balance some conflicting criteria. In practice, however, stakeholders must translate their qualitative preferences between different criteria into numerical weights, priorities, or reference points required by optimization methods, which can be difficult to specify and interpret.\nL765: This paper proposes \\emph{ParetoChat}, an LLM (large language model)-based expert-agent interface for interactive multi-objective optimization. The agent translates natural-language user input into structured scalarization parameters, invokes an optimization system, presents candidate solutions, and iteratively elicits feedback. We describe a multi-agent architecture that separates input processing, multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM) expertise, solution presentation, and feedback processing.\nL766: We then present a simulation study using an LLM-based DM agent and benchmark ParetoChat against interactive methods implemented in DESDEO, including reference-point and NIMBUS methods. The computational experiments on multi-objective binary knapsack and set-partitioning instances show that ParetoChat can guide the search efficiently toward solutions that match the DM's preferences.\nL767: An application to historical courier-order assignment instances illustrates how users can explore objective extremes and intermediate trade-offs through natural-language requests. The results indicate that LLM agents may provide a practical interaction layer between business stakeholders and optimization tools.\nL768: [61] cite531†arXiv:2610.23071 [cite532†pdf , cite533†html , cite534†other ]\nL769: \nL770: \nL771: Title: Before Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents\nL772: \nL773: cite535†Aditya Nawal , cite536†Manit Baser , cite537†Mohan Gurusamy L774: \nL775: Comments: 19 pages, 5 figures. Preprint version of a manuscript submitted to Transactions of the Association for Computational Linguistics (TACL)\nL776: \nL777: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL778: Large language models increasingly act as agents that call external tools, yet most tool-safety research examines only what follows a tool call. The prior decision of whether to call the tool remains largely unexamined and can influence downstream safety. Across the seven open-weight models, the unweighted model-average harm rate after admitted harmful requests is 74.5%. To identify what governs this decision, we apply a factorial intervention to the input request.\nL779: Within our generated construction, neutralizing the requested-operation component produces the largest admission contrast (19 pp). A linear direction estimated from this contrast influences held-out admission in four of seven models; effects on operation-neutralized requests in some models show that it is not an operation-specific mechanism. We then test whether written refusal is behaviorally and representationally coupled to the same decision. Written refusal accompanies 33.6% of admitted harmful calls.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputation and Language (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new)\nciteturn41200view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn41184view0\",\"lineno\":1000}); Total lines: 2886\nL982: Even at ten GPU-hours per run, reward collection alone would consume hundreds of thousands of GPU-hours, motivating a cheaper surrogate. Second, surrogate feedback can become unreliable as policy optimization favors overestimated proposals when the proposal distribution shifts. To address these challenges, we propose LM2LM, which trains LLMs with RL to generate effective LM improvement proposals.\nL983: To provide scalable feedback, we use a research world model (RWM), a frozen LLM that predicts proposal gains from real experimental records provided in context, as the reward model for RL. To maintain feedback reliability, we periodically execute current-policy proposals ranked highly by the RWM and add their measured outcomes to its context.\nL984: Across eight autoregressive and diffusion LM development environments, real-execution evaluations on held-out tasks show that LM2LM enables Qwen3.8-27B to outperform GPT-6-sol, even when GPT-6-sol is given 300 real experimental records in context, while requiring 33% lower data-generation cost. LM2LM also generalizes to a model family unseen during training.\nL985: [79] cite677†arXiv:2610.23388 [cite678†pdf , cite679†html , cite680†other ]\nL986: \nL987: \nL988: Title: Sentence-Level Topic Modeling with Lexical and Contextual Evidence\nL989: \nL990: cite681†Federica Granese , cite682†Serena Villata , cite683†Charles Bouveyron L991: \nL992: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL993: Topic modeling faces a fundamental granularity dilemma: probabilistic topic models assign topics at the word level, whereas transformer-based methods operate at the document level. The latter overlooks that real-world documents typically cover multiple topics, whereas the former relies on individual words, which rarely provide sufficient context to identify them. We introduce SentenceETM, a neural topic model that assigns a topic to each sentence by jointly using lexical and contextual evidence.\nL994: Each topic is described both by a distribution over the vocabulary, defined through word embeddings, and by a Gaussian over sentence embeddings concentrated on a topic-specific low-dimensional subspace. The topic of a sentence generates both its words and its embedding and is marginalized exactly, so that its posterior combines the two sources of evidence. Document proportions are inferred with amortized variational inference, while the Gaussian parameters are re-estimated in closed form.\nL995: Experiments show that SentenceETM consistently improves sentence-level topic assignment over state-of-the-art approaches, and a case study of a presidential debate shows how sentence-level topics reveal how candidates handle the same subjects.\nL996: [80] cite684†arXiv:2610.23400 [cite685†pdf , cite686†html , cite687†other ]\nL997: \nL998: \nL999: Title: On the Tip of the Tongue: First-Token Cueing as a Behavioral Probe of Recall Failures in LLMs\nL1000: \nL1001: cite688†Jaewon Sok , cite689†Jewon Yeom , cite690†Seonghyeon Park , cite691†Jeongjae Park , cite692†Hwiyeong Lee , cite693†Taesup Kim L1002: \nL1003: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL1004: Large language models often answer factual questions incorrectly in closed-book settings, and such errors are usually attributed to missing knowledge. Yet models often encode answers they fail to produce. We show that many closed-book errors are first-token recall failures: the model commits to a wrong first token, but once given the right one it completes the answer and often recognizes it as correct.\nL1005: We study this with First-Token Cueing, a behavioral probe that prefills the response with the first token of the gold answer. Model uncertainty peaks at the first answer token, where it already predicts correctness, and among gold prefixes of any length the first token alone accounts for the largest share of recovered errors.\nL1006: Across 16 open models, this one token corrects between a fifth and a half of the errors on multi-token answers in English QA and improves accuracy in all seven languages of a multilingual benchmark.\nL1007: Most of the errors it corrects are also solved by the model's own sampled reasoning, a criterion for encoded knowledge, and models recognize correct starts they do not produce: they continue a correct start more confidently than an incorrect start of the same answer type and prior probability, yet rarely find it without the gold answer. These results locate a recoverable class of factual errors at the first decoding step, where inference-time methods may gain the most.\nL1008: [81] cite694†arXiv:2610.23455 [cite695†pdf , cite696†html , cite697†other ]\nL1009: \nL1010: \nL1011: Title: HydraLLM: Adaptive Width Slicing for Elastic Text and Vision-Language Models\nL1012: \nL1013: cite698†Oscar Kemme , cite699†Janek Haberer , cite700†Ali Hojjat , cite701†Olaf Landsiedel L1014: \nL1015: Comments: Accepted at the BabyLM Workshop at EMNLP 2026\nL1016: \nL1017: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL1018: LLMs and LVLMs are increasingly deployed across heterogeneous hardware, ranging from data-center GPUs to resource-constrained edge devices. Their fixed-size architectures limit the ability to reuse a single model across deployment settings with different compute, latency, and model-size targets. We introduce HydraLLM, an elastic MQA-based architecture.\nL1019: Its number of active attention heads determines the effective widths of the embeddings, attention projections, feed-forward layers, and the output head, forming a nested family of subnetworks within a single parameter set. We introduce a static-to-elastic conversion pipeline that, starting from a pre-trained model checkpoint, includes importance-driven weight reordering and stochastic subnetwork training with knowledge distillation.\nL1020: We extend this principle to multimodal inference through an elastic projection module whose output width follows the active text width. HydraLLM covers a wider range of active parameter configurations than FFN-only elastic architectures. With 75% of heads active, HydraLLM achieves 2.2 percentage points higher TriviaQA accuracy than a state-of-the-art nested baseline configuration which has 17.25% more parameters.\nL1021: With 50% of heads active, our multimodal model achieves a CLIPScore comparable to that of the 7$\\times$ larger Gemma 3 4B model. With only 25% of heads active, HydraLLM increases token-generation throughput by 75.3% over the full-width configuration.\nL1022: [82] cite702†arXiv:2610.23456 [cite703†pdf , cite704†html , cite705†other ]\nL1023: \nL1024: \nL1025: Title: A low-dimensional dynamical basis for tongue shape control\nL1026: \nL1027: cite706†Sam Kirkham , cite707†Patrycja Strycharczuk L1028: \nL1029: Comments: Pre-print currently under review\nL1030: \nL1031: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL1032: Speech production involves the control of a high-dimensional articulatory system with many degrees-of-freedom, but a widespread hypothesis is that speakers navigate this complexity using a set of lower-dimensional control variables. Common candidates for these control variables include individual articulators (e.g. tongue body) and abstract tract variables (e.g. tongue body constriction location), but identifying the most appropriate coordinate system for articulatory control remains an open question.\nL1033: In this study, we advance a model in which holistic tongue shape modes represent a potential low-dimensional control basis for a simple dynamical law. We first discover empirical tongue shape modes in a cross-speaker coordinate space and then fit the model to ultrasound data on monophthongal vowels from 36 speakers of British English. A three-dimensional linear point attractor fitted to these time-varying modes recovers the dynamics of tongue shape with a high degree of accuracy.\nL1034: This suggests that complex tongue movement can arise from independent modes that linearly combine under simple dynamical laws, thereby relocating some complexity from the control signal to the coordinate system. We show that this framing explains why one-dimensional articulatory measures can exhibit observable nonlinearities, and hypothesize that shape modes are a plausible kinematic expression of underlying muscle synergies.\nL1035: [83] cite708†arXiv:2610.23480 [cite709†pdf , cite710†html , cite711†other ]\nL1036: \nL1037: \nL1038: Title: MuninAI: A Self-Hosted Agentic Framework for Independent Research Groups\nL1039: \nL1040: cite712†Markus Fischer , cite713†Jens Meiler , cite714†Matthias Elgeti L1041: \nL1042: Comments: 37 pages, 3 figures, 20 tables. Code, evaluation suite and scorecards: cite715†this https URL†github.com (archived release v1.0.0: cite716†this https URL†doi.org )\nL1043: \nL1044: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n"
